<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">creatures</ansi>

Not every companion is a person. A creature takes one company slot and one
formation cell like anyone else, and fights by its own strategy, but it is
kept differently. Two kinds are on offer in the Dunmar region:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Hound</ansi>         a biological creature (<ansi fg="command">help hound</ansi>)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Stone Golem</ansi>   a construct (<ansi fg="command">help stone-golem</ansi>)

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What every creature shares</ansi>

  - It carries nothing: no starter pack, no share of the company's load, no
    horse to ride. It wears only gear cut for its own kind, and no person can
    wear that gear (<ansi fg="command">help equipment</ansi>).
  - It has ranks that come with its level, but no promotion route and no
    talents (<ansi fg="command">help classes</ansi>).
  - It cannot be given a different archetype, and no player can choose to be
    one (<ansi fg="command">company archetype</ansi> refuses it).
  - It is recruited, not trained: <ansi fg="command">company recruit</ansi> at a recruiter lists
    it on a "Creature:" line (<ansi fg="command">help company</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What differs</ansi>

                        Hound                    Stone Golem
  Food, drink, rest     like any companion       none needed
  Mends                 by rest, camp and inn    only with stone mortar
  Loses heart, leaves   yes, like anyone         never (it is bound)
  Takes a bed at an inn yes                      no

Constructs show as <ansi fg="command">bound</ansi> instead of a loyalty number in <ansi fg="command">company status</ansi>, and
<ansi fg="command">survival</ansi> says they need nothing. To mend one, see <ansi fg="command">help repair</ansi>.
